{"ex-3B05-1":{"number":"1","title":"Wave pattern produced in a ripple tank","question":"<p>The figure shows a wave pattern formed by a ripple tank with a constant water depth. Point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is the position of the dipper.</p><p>(a) Draw an arrow to show the travelling direction of the wave at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) The frequency of the dipper is 6 Hz. Find the speed of the water wave.</p><p>(c) The vibrating frequency of the dipper is now doubled. How will the wave speed and the wavelength change?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/80336f3a13070f297482e874.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:199.4px\"/></p><p>Explain briefly.</p>","answer":"<p>(a)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/0c1a2e1fc7c155ac48351c0c.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:280.7px\"/></p><p>(b) Wavelength λ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 4 cm = 0.04 m</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>λ = 6 × 0.04 = 0.24 m s<sup>−1</sup></p><p>(c) Since the water depth is unchanged, the wave speed remains unchanged.</p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>λ , for constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, λ will be reduced by half as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is doubled.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B05  |  Source PDF p. 6","source":{"name":"3B05 Examples.docx","sha256":"eae90833c5744372abd761de5c3d8e15e3e5cf92d7d863432ea75f5c155d698a","block":2},"id":"ex-3B05-1","chapter":"3B05"},"ex-3B05-2":{"number":"2","title":"A wave entering a deep region","question":"<p>A straight water wave enters a deep region from a shallow region as shown.</p><p>(a) Does the wave travel faster or slower in the deep region?</p><p>(b) Draw the wave pattern in the</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/2dcc764fa00c74afe626f751.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:253.4px\"/></p><p>deep region.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Faster</p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/ac5987d78f42f70684315f36.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:253.4px\"/></p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B05  |  Source PDF p. 13","source":{"name":"3B05 Examples.docx","sha256":"eae90833c5744372abd761de5c3d8e15e3e5cf92d7d863432ea75f5c155d698a","block":17},"id":"ex-3B05-2","chapter":"3B05"},"ex-3B05-3":{"number":"3","title":"Refraction of a wave","question":"<p>A straight water wave travels from region <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to region <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> as shown.</p><p>(a) When the wave crosses the boundary,</p><p>(i) how does its frequency change?</p><p>(ii) does it bend towards or away from the normal?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/dec50dd4e6cb7ccd2158326b.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:270.0px\"/></p><p>(b) Which region is deeper?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) (i) Its frequency does not change.</p><p>(ii) It bends towards the normal.</p><p>(b) Region <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B05  |  Source PDF p. 15","source":{"name":"3B05 Examples.docx","sha256":"eae90833c5744372abd761de5c3d8e15e3e5cf92d7d863432ea75f5c155d698a","block":28},"id":"ex-3B05-3","chapter":"3B05"},"ex-3B05-4":{"number":"4","title":"Snell’s law","question":"<p>A straight water wave travels from region <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> to region <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> as shown. The angle of incidence is 30° and the angle of refraction is 20°.</p><p>(a) Find the wavelength λ<sub>X</sub> of the wave in region <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) How will the angle of refraction</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/1539d4d824840a8dfde28883.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:212.3px\"/></p><p>change if the water depth in region <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is reduced?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) λ<sub>Y</sub> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = 5 cm</p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>°</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>5</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></p><p>λ<sub>X</sub> = 7.31 cm</p><p>(b) Decrease</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>i</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>θ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>,</p><p>water depth in Y ↓</p><p>⇒ v<sub>Y</sub> ↓ ⇒ sin θ<sub>Y</sub> ↓ ⇒ θ<sub>Y</sub> ↓</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B05  |  Source PDF p. 18","source":{"name":"3B05 Examples.docx","sha256":"eae90833c5744372abd761de5c3d8e15e3e5cf92d7d863432ea75f5c155d698a","block":40},"id":"ex-3B05-4","chapter":"3B05"},"ex-3B05-5":{"number":"5","title":"Determining the type of interference","question":"<p>The figure shows two dippers placed at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. They vibrate in phase to produce circular water waves of wavelength 4 cm in a ripple tank.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/9afea0714946e0923bec4bfc.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:280.9px\"/></p><p>(a) Determine the type of interference at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) If the wavelength of the wave is doubled, how will the type of interference at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> be affected?</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Path difference at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 31 – 19 = 12 cm = 3λ</p><p>∴ Constructive interference occurs at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) New wavelength = 2 × 4 = 8 cm</p><p>Path difference at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 12 cm = 1.5λ</p><p>∴ Destructive interference will occur at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>R</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","tips":"","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B05  |  Source PDF p. 31","source":{"name":"3B05 Examples.docx","sha256":"eae90833c5744372abd761de5c3d8e15e3e5cf92d7d863432ea75f5c155d698a","block":58},"id":"ex-3B05-5","chapter":"3B05"},"ex-3B05-6":{"number":"6","title":"Interference pattern","question":"<p>The figure shows water waves of wavelength λ produced by sources <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> vibrating in phase. The purple lines and green lines represent wave crests.</p><p>(a) Find the path differences at points <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> in terms of λ.</p><p>(b) What type of interference occurs at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>?</p><p>(c) If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 15 cm and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is 11 cm, find λ.</p><p>(d) Describe the motion of a particle at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at this instant.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/5621c7ecec4b0859762af2a0.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:152.6px\"/></p>","answer":"<p>(a) Path difference at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = path difference at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>P</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 5λ − 3λ = 2λ</p><p>(b) Constructive interference</p><p>(c) Δ = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> − <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>Q</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>2λ = 15 − 11</p><p>λ = 2 cm</p><p>(d) It is momentarily at rest.</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>P and Q lie on the same antinodal line, so they have the same Δ.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>It is at the crest at this instant. It will then move downwards to the trough and then back to the crest in one period.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B05  |  Source PDF p. 33","source":{"name":"3B05 Examples.docx","sha256":"eae90833c5744372abd761de5c3d8e15e3e5cf92d7d863432ea75f5c155d698a","block":70},"id":"ex-3B05-6","chapter":"3B05"},"ex-3B05-7":{"number":"7","title":"Interference and vibrations","question":"<p>Two dippers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> vibrate in phase and produce water waves of wavelength 2 cm (Fig a). The green lines represent wave crests. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is the perpendicular bisector of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, maxima occur at some points and minima occur at some other points.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/a153687b3983044e1fbbe3b2.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:515.1px\"/></p><p>(a) Explain why a maximum occurs at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(b) Point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is the second minimum from point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Find the path difference (in cm) at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>C</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(c) Sketch how the amplitude of the water wave varies along the line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>A</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>(d) Figure b shows the variation of the displacement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>s</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> due to each wave with time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> at a certain point on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. Do the two waves arrive at this point in phase?</p><p>(e) If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> vibrate in antiphase, will a maximum or a minimum occur at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>? Explain.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) The path difference at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> is zero, so constructive interference occurs.</p><p>(b) Path difference Δ = 1.5λ = 1.5(2) = 3 cm</p><p>(c)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/ad13f9fe42ad736a43b22f10.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:270.9px\"/></p><p>(d) No</p><p>(e) The waves from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>S</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> arrive <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> in antiphase.</p><p>∴ Destructive interference always occurs.</p><p>∴ A minimum will occur at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>B</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p><p>Practice 5.3 Q6 (p.75)</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>For 1st min, Δ = 0.5λ</p><p>For 2nd min, Δ = 1.5λ</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The amplitude of a circular wave decreases gradually with the distance from the source.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>The crests of the two waves do not arrive at the same time.</p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>A maximum will become a minimum, and vice versa.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B05  |  Source PDF p. 36","source":{"name":"3B05 Examples.docx","sha256":"eae90833c5744372abd761de5c3d8e15e3e5cf92d7d863432ea75f5c155d698a","block":90},"id":"ex-3B05-7","chapter":"3B05"},"ex-3B05-8":{"number":"8","title":"Wavelength, frequency and number of loops","question":"<p>A student produces stationary waves with 1, 2 and 3 loops on an elastic string as shown. The speed of waves along the string is 10.8 m s<sup>−1</sup>.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/d7a6298b8064d597d3fcc4d6.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:566.4px\"/></p><p>Find the wavelength and frequency of each wave. Hence, suggest the relationship between the frequency of the wave <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and the number of loops <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","answer":"<p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/7d26b8ed1039fd25651aafa7.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:566.4px\"/></p><div class=\"study-table-wrap\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Number of loop (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>)</p></td><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p></td><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p></td><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Wavelength (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>)</p></td><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p></td><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p></td><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>×</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>cm</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Frequency (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>). By <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>,</p></td><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Hz</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p></td><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>9</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Hz</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p></td><td><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>0</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>.</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>6</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>8</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>Hz</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext> </mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>(</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>3</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Therefore, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math></p>","tips":"","think":"<p>Can you express λₙ in terms of the length of the string L and the number of loops n?</p>","thinkAnswer":"<p>Each loop has length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. With both ends fixed, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>. Consequently <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>λ</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>v</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>2</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>L</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mrow><mtext>=</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>n</mtext></mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>f</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>1</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</p>","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B05  |  Source PDF p. 45","source":{"name":"3B05 Examples.docx","sha256":"eae90833c5744372abd761de5c3d8e15e3e5cf92d7d863432ea75f5c155d698a","block":119},"id":"ex-3B05-8","chapter":"3B05"},"ex-3B05-9":{"number":"9","title":"Particle motion on a wave","question":"<p>The figure shows a wave on a string at time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math></p><p>and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are particles on the string. At this instant, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Z</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> are both moving downwards.</p><p>(a) Is this a travelling wave or stationary wave?</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/2d5dbe5717fd4cf480aefc4c.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:113.2px\"/></p><p>(b) The period of the wave is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>. On the same figure, sketch the</p><p>displacement−time graphs for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>X</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = 0 to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>t</mtext></mrow></mrow></math> = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>T</mtext></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>","answer":"<p>(a) Stationary wave</p><p>(b)</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/75fb7201898d246977492ef8.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:224.5px\"/></p><p>Revision 5 Q29 (p.96)</p>","tips":"<p><b>Tip</b></p><p>If this were a travelling wave, X and Z would move in opposite directions.</p><p class=\"study-figure\"><img src=\"assets/textbook-examples/media/54ab6938587699a717c983f6.png\" alt=\"\" style=\"width:179.4px\"></p><p><b>Tip</b></p><p>Since X is moving downwards at t = 0, it is not at the maximum displacement. s<sub>X</sub> is smaller than the amplitude and is decreasing. As Y vibrates in phase with X, its s−t graph has the same shape but with a larger amplitude.</p>","think":"","thinkAnswer":"","location":"Book 3B  |  Chapter 3B05  |  Source PDF p. 48","source":{"name":"3B05 Examples.docx","sha256":"eae90833c5744372abd761de5c3d8e15e3e5cf92d7d863432ea75f5c155d698a","block":132},"id":"ex-3B05-9","chapter":"3B05"}}